vanadis: return the caller's TID from set_tid_address

Linux returns the thread ID of the caller from set_tid_address(2), and
musl and glibc store that value as the TID of the main thread.  Vanadis
returned 0, so every program started with a TID of 0.

musl 1.2.6 made exit() thread-safe with a lock: exit() does a
compare-and-swap of its own TID into a lock word that starts at 0, and
calls a_crash() when the old value equals its TID, to trap a recursive
call.  With a TID of 0, the first call to exit() matches and crashes.
Vanadis then stops at the ebreak of a_crash() with "instruction-type=
DECODE_FAULT", as in the rdmaNic tests app/rdma/msg and the others
built with the riscv64 musl cross compiler of the dev shell.

Return the TID of the process, as the gettid syscall does.  Vanadis
assigns TIDs from 100, so the value is never 0.  Programs built with
older musl versions or with glibc get the value that Linux gives them.

--- a/src/sst/elements/vanadis/os/syscall/settidaddress.h
+++ b/src/sst/elements/vanadis/os/syscall/settidaddress.h
@@ -32,7 +32,9 @@
         #endif
         process->setTidAddress( event->getTidAddress() );

-        setReturnSuccess(0);
+        // Linux returns the TID of the caller.  musl keeps it as the TID of
+        // the main thread, and its exit() crashes when that TID is 0.
+        setReturnSuccess(process->gettid());
     }
 };

